WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.296
Replay risk exists when a previously valid event is reused outside its
intended time or context.

2
00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:10.396
A signature may still match an old payload, especially if the signing
secret has not changed.

3
00:00:10.396 --> 00:00:20.856
Freshness controls such as timestamps, bounded acceptance windows, event
identifiers, request identifiers, or nonce concepts help the consumer
decide whether an event is plausible now.

4
00:00:20.856 --> 00:00:25.760
These controls reduce the chance that old events can be accepted as new
business signals.

5
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.702
Freshness and idempotency are related but separate.

6
00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:33.213
Freshness asks whether an event should still be accepted based on time or
context.

7
00:00:33.213 --> 00:00:41.125
Idempotency asks whether processing the same intended event more than once
creates the same outcome without unwanted duplicate side effects.

8
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:46.617
A handler can reject stale events and still need idempotency for
legitimate duplicate deliveries.

9
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:51.782
Conversely, deduplication alone does not prove that a very old event
should still be honored.

10
00:00:51.782 --> 00:00:54.528
Duplicate delivery is normal in webhook systems.

11
00:00:54.528 --> 00:01:04.269
Providers often retry when they do not receive a timely success response,
and networks can fail after the provider has sent the event but before it
receives the acknowledgement.

12
00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.473
Exactly-once delivery is rarely a practical assumption.

13
00:01:07.473 --> 00:01:22.118
Python handlers should use event identifiers, idempotency keys,
deduplication records, state checks, or transaction-aware processing to
avoid duplicate charges, repeated approvals, duplicate tickets, repeated
messages, or conflicting state transitions.

14
00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:24.080
Timeout ambiguity is the hard case.

15
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:30.618
If a worker times out while processing an event, the application may not
know whether the downstream action completed.

16
00:01:30.618 --> 00:01:35.587
A secure design defines reconciliation behavior, durable records, and safe
retry rules.

17
00:01:35.587 --> 00:01:40.686
Handlers should be able to resume or ignore work based on recorded state
rather than guesses.

18
00:01:40.686 --> 00:01:46.440
The goal is to make repeated delivery safe while still ensuring legitimate
events are not silently lost.
